WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.309 --> 00:00:06.871
Weeks passed and I regained my health, but no mention was made of sending me to school.

2
00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:13.635
Mrs. Reed favored her own children more than ever, and she ordered them not to speak to me.

3
00:00:13.636 --> 00:00:25.948
One day John tried to hit me, but I struck first, punching him in the nose as hard as I could. He went blubbering to his mama while I ran upstairs.

4
00:00:25.949 --> 00:00:35.283
I heard him telling a tale of how "that nasty Jane Eyre" had flown at him like a mad cat, but he was interrupted rather harshly.

5
00:00:35.284 --> 00:00:47.519
"Don't talk to me about her, John," said Mrs. Reed. "I told you not to go near her. I don't want either you or your sisters to associate with her."

6
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:54.527
Leaning over the banister, I cried out, "They're not fit to associate with me!"

7
00:00:54.528 --> 00:01:02.335
Mrs. Reed was a rather stout woman, but on hearing this bold declaration, she ran nimbly upstairs.

8
00:01:02.336 --> 00:01:11.067
She swept me into the nursery and forbade me to rise from my bed or to utter one more syllable that day.

9
00:01:11.068 --> 00:01:15.422
"What would my uncle say if he were alive?" I demanded.

10
00:01:15.423 --> 00:01:19.482
"What?" Mrs. Reed suddenly seemed fearful.

11
00:01:19.483 --> 00:01:31.819
"My uncle Reed is in heaven, and so are Papa and Mama," I went on boldly. "They can see all you do and think. They know how you wish me dead."

12
00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:42.894
Mrs. Reed shook me most soundly. She left me without saying another word, but Bessie lectured me for an hour on what a wicked child I was.

13
00:01:42.895 --> 00:01:49.314
Christmas and the New Year were celebrated at Gateshead Hall with the usual festive cheer.

14
00:01:49.315 --> 00:01:59.303
Presents were exchanged; dinners and evening parties were given. Of course I was excluded from every happy moment.

15
00:01:59.304 --> 00:02:05.215
Some evenings when there was company, Bessie would bring me a special treat.

16
00:02:05.216 --> 00:02:11.791
She sat on my bed while I ate, and when I finished, she tucked the covers around me.

17
00:02:11.792 --> 00:02:18.818
Bessie was often quick to anger, but when she was gentle, she was my favorite person.

18
00:02:18.819 --> 00:02:22.604
It was now the morning of January fifteenth.

19
00:02:22.605 --> 00:02:32.432
Eliza was heading outdoors while Georgiana sat in front of the mirror, adorning her hair with artificial flowers she'd found in the attic.

20
00:02:32.433 --> 00:02:40.020
I was making my bed because Bessie frequently employed me as a sort of nursery maid to tidy the room.

21
00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:51.498
Having finished my work, I stood in front of the window. I saw a carriage roll up the drive, but of more interest to me was a hungry robin perched in a tree.

22
00:02:51.499 --> 00:03:00.326
I crumbled some roll left from my breakfast and was just about to toss it out the window when Bessie came running into the nursery.

23
00:03:00.327 --> 00:03:07.906
"What are you doing, Miss Jane?" she cried breathlessly. "You're wanted in the sitting room."

24
00:03:07.907 --> 00:03:15.158
Bessie quickly scrubbed my hands and face, ran a brush through my hair, and removed my pinafore.

25
00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:24.065
"Who could want me?" I wondered as I stood outside the sitting room. Mrs. Reed hadn't summoned me for months.

26
00:03:24.066 --> 00:03:30.706
I entered the room. Mrs. Reed occupied her usual seat by the fireside.

27
00:03:30.707 --> 00:03:37.055
Standing next to her was a tall, severe man dressed entirely in black.

28
00:03:37.056 --> 00:03:43.458
I curtsied and Mrs. Reed said, "This is the little girl that I wrote to you about."

29
00:03:43.459 --> 00:03:50.282
The man scrutinized me for some minutes before addressing me. "Your name, little girl?"

30
00:03:50.283 --> 00:03:52.386
"Jane Eyre, sir." "Well,

31
00:03:52.387 --> 00:03:55.956
Jane Eyre, are you a good child?" Mrs.

32
00:03:55.956 --> 00:04:04.864
Reed answered for me. "Perhaps the less said on that subject the better, Mr. Brocklehurst." "I'm

33
00:04:04.865 --> 00:04:14.420
sorry indeed to hear that." Bending from the waist, he installed himself in the armchair opposite Mrs. Reed. "Come here." I

34
00:04:14.421 --> 00:04:24.074
crossed the rug and he placed me right in front of him. What a large face he had now that it was almost level with mine! "There

35
00:04:24.075 --> 00:04:35.021
is no sight so sad as a naughty child," he began, "especially a naughty little girl. Do you know where the wicked go after they die?" "They

36
00:04:35.022 --> 00:04:39.112
go to hell" was my ready and expected answer. "What

37
00:04:39.113 --> 00:04:42.252
must you do to avoid hell?" I

38
00:04:42.253 --> 00:04:50.658
thought for a moment, and when I gave my answer, I knew it was the wrong one. "I must not die." Mr.

39
00:04:50.659 --> 00:04:57.620
Brocklehurst shook his head sadly. "I fear that you won't go to heaven." I

40
00:04:57.621 --> 00:05:05.872
looked down at his two large feet planted on the rug and sighed, wishing myself far away. "Do

41
00:05:05.872 --> 00:05:10.326
you say your prayers morning and evening?" he asked. "Yes,

42
00:05:10.327 --> 00:05:11.825
sir." "Do

43
00:05:11.826 --> 00:05:14.137
you read your Bible?" "Sometimes."

44
00:05:14.138 --> 00:05:15.875
"Are

45
00:05:15.876 --> 00:05:20.504
you fond of it? I hope you like the Psalms." "Psalms

46
00:05:20.505 --> 00:05:23.848
are not interesting," I said. "That

47
00:05:23.848 --> 00:05:29.286
proves you have a wicked heart," concluded Mr. Brocklehurst. Mrs.

48
00:05:29.287 --> 00:05:34.680
Reed told me to sit down, and then she carried on the conversation. "Mr.

49
00:05:34.681 --> 00:05:43.110
Brocklehurst, I believe I told you in my letter that Jane does not have quite the disposition that I would wish. If

50
00:05:43.111 --> 00:05:52.111
you admit her to Lowood school, I'd be glad if you requested that the teachers keep an eye on her because she is deceitful. I

51
00:05:52.112 --> 00:05:58.372
mention this in front of you, Jane, so that you don't try to deceive Mr. Brocklehurst." She

52
00:05:58.373 --> 00:06:05.911
was already wiping hope from my new existence. I felt tears springing to my eyes. "Deceit

53
00:06:05.912 --> 00:06:18.584
is indeed a sad fault in a child," Mr. Brocklehurst said. "It's the same as a falsehood, and all liars go to hell. I will speak to the teachers." "I

54
00:06:18.585 --> 00:06:23.414
want her to be brought up humbly," continued Mrs. Reed. Mr.

55
00:06:23.415 --> 00:06:35.815
Brocklehurst nodded. "My own daughter recently commented on how quiet and plain all the girls at Lowood look with their hair combed behind their ears and their long pinafores." Then

56
00:06:35.816 --> 00:06:44.142
they settled that I would be transported to Lowood as soon as possible and that I would also spend all my vacations there. As

57
00:06:44.143 --> 00:06:49.544
he left, Mr. Brocklehurst handed me a thin book. "Read

58
00:06:49.545 --> 00:07:02.526
it with prayer," he advised, "especially that part containing an account of the awfully sudden death of Martha G., a naughty child addicted to falsehood and deceit." He

59
00:07:02.527 --> 00:07:13.116
left, and Mrs. Reed ordered me back to the nursery. I walked to the door, but then I turned back to Mrs. Reed. I had to speak. "I'm

60
00:07:13.117 --> 00:07:26.346
not deceitful. If I were, I'd say I love you, but I declare I don't love you. As for this book, you should give it to Georgiana because she tells lies. People

61
00:07:26.347 --> 00:07:33.092
think you are a good woman, but you are bad, and I will tell that to everyone at Lowood." Mrs.

62
00:07:33.093 --> 00:07:38.597
Reed looked frightened. "What's wrong with you, dear?" "I

63
00:07:38.598 --> 00:07:47.008
am not your dear . Send me to school soon, Mrs. Reed, for I hate living here!"

